    initials: String.t(), presence: :online | :away | :offline}.
    Renders <img> when image_url is present, initials div otherwise.
    ```
      </public-surface>
      <private-surface>
    ```
    presence_ring_class(presence) -> String.t() — CSS class for the
    presence-colored ring.
    ```
      </private-surface>
      <processes>
      </processes>
      <dependencies>
      </dependencies>
    </ui_subcomparch>

# Rules

## Structure

* Emit **exactly one** `<ui_subcomparch>` root block. The five
  children **must appear in this order**: `<technical-specification>`
  → `<public-surface>` → `<private-surface>` → `<processes>` →
  `<dependencies>` → `<references>` (omit entirely when nothing is
  attached).

## Fragment sections

* `<technical-specification>` is a role-level paragraph narrowing the
  parent collection's techspec to this leaf's own slice — don't
  restate the parent verbatim.
* `<public-surface>` and `<private-surface>` follow the signature
  discipline every leaf tier in this chain uses: every callable entry
  shows its prop/parameter shape, whether it's a pure render or has a
  side effect (rare here — most UI leaves have none), and any slot
  contract. **No backend call, no fetch, no subscription belongs in
  either surface** — that is the layering violation this whole family
  exists to make structurally absent.

## Processes

* `<processes>` is almost always empty at this tier — a presentational
  leaf runs inside its caller's process. Declare a named process only
  when this leaf genuinely owns standing state distinct from its
  caller (a client-side debounce timer implemented as its own process,
  for instance) — the same `local`/`singleton`/`sharded` categories and
  guardrail discipline every `*_subcomparch` tier in this chain uses.
  Emit `<processes></processes>` empty otherwise.

## Dependencies

* `<dependencies>` lists sibling leaves within the same UI collection
  this one composes, each `<dep to="..."/>` with a real id from the
  list above. Almost always empty or very small — most rendering
  decomposition is a parent composing children directly through slots,
  not leaves calling across to each other.

## References

* `<references>` is optional — omit entirely when nothing is attached.

## Meta-rules

* Do not include commentary about what you are doing or how you
  arrived at the design. Output only the `<ui_subcomparch>` block.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` inside fragment-section text are tolerated.
* Do not emit `<policies>`, `<subcomponents>`, or `<sub-dependencies>`
  — leaves in this tier don't introduce new policies and can't
  decompose further.
